    "lesson_id": "036",
    "trigger_word": "kubadili",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 36: IT BROKE: THE STATIVE",
    "header_color": "yellow",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Things change, spoil, come apart —",
          "often with no one to blame. The",
          "stative -ik-/-ek- says it happened",
          "of itself, or that it CAN happen:",
          "",
          "   kuvunja    to break (something)",
          "   kuvunjika  to break, be broken",
          "",
          "   Kikombe kimevunjika.",
          "   The cup is broken. (Lesson 15",
          "   had Imevunjika: now explained.)",
          "",
          "   kuharibika   to spoil, go bad",
          "   kubadilika   to change (of itself)",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Kuvunjika. Kikombe kimevunjika. Kuharibika."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Stative against passive — the pair",
          "that proves the system:",
          "",
          "   Kikombe kilivunjWA.",
          "   The cup was broken (by someone —",
          "   there is an act, and an actor).",
          "",
          "   Kikombe kilivunjIKA.",
          "   The cup broke (state; no one",
          "   blamed, no one named).",
          "",
          "Hear the pair:                     Press 1",
          "",
          "Swahili speakers reach for -ika",
          "constantly — it is the polite",
          "physics of everyday life: things",
          "simply happen."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Kilivunjwa. Kilivunjika."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
